      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "PURPOSE",
          "content": "To review the long term clinical outcomes in the treatment of osteoid osteoma (OO) using radiofrequency ablation (RFA)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MATERIALS AND METHODS",
          "content": "Our retrospective study included 59 patients who were treated in the period from April 2001 to December 2012 due to a symptomatic OO using RFA. Here, the occurrence of complications and postoperative recurrence, as well as postoperative patient satisfaction were examined. Patients satisfaction was assessed by means of a telephone interview with the visual analogue scale (VAS)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Mean follow-up was 50 months (2 \u2013116 months). The average size of the nidus was 6mm (range 2 \u2013 14 mm). After initial radiofrequency ablation 11.8 % (7/59) of patient showed a recurrence of symptoms. Symptoms could successfully be treated by a second ablation in 5 patients. Assisted success rate was therefore 96.6 % (57/59). The complication rate was 5.1 % (2 major and one minor complication). Furthermore we report a very high patient satisfaction and acceptance of therapy."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "RFA is a very successful therapy of symptomatic OOs with a high patient satisfaction."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "KEY POINTS",
          "content": "Osteoid osteomas (OO) are rare benign bone tumors of the childhood and adolescence. Treatment of OOs with minimal-invasive radiofrequency ablation (RFA) shows a high patient satisfaction. RFA is by now the standard therapy of symptomatic OOs."
        }
      ]},
